        "marks": 5,
        "cognitive_level": "U",
        "raw_text": "Describe the cleaning action of soaps and what happens during the formation of micelles.",
        "content": {
          "stem": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Describe the cleaning action of soaps and what happens during the formation of micelles."
            }
          ]
        },
        "topic_names": [
          "4.5 SOAPS AND DETERGENTS"
        ],
        "answer": "Soap molecules have two distinct ends: a hydrophilic ionic end that interacts with water and a hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail that interacts with oil. Most dirt is oily and does not dissolve in water. When soap is added, the hydrocarbon tails attach to the oil droplets, while the ionic ends face the surrounding water. This orientation pulls the dirt away from the surface and forms a structure called a micelle. These micelles stay in the water as a colloid due to ion-ion repulsion, forming an emulsion that can be easily rinsed away, leaving clothes clean.",
