            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compare the effectiveness of soap and detergent in hard water, explaining why they differ in performance."
            }
          ]
        },
        "topic_names": [
          "4.5 SOAPS AND DETERGENTS"
        ],
        "answer": "Soap is ineffective in hard water because it contains calcium and magnesium ions that react with soap molecules to form an insoluble substance known as scum. This results in the formation of foam with difficulty and requires using more soap. Conversely, detergents are typically sodium salts of sulphonic acids or ammonium salts with chlorides/bromides. These compounds do not form insoluble precipitates with the calcium or magnesium ions present in hard water. Therefore, detergents remain effective cleansing agents in hard water and are widely used in shampoos and clothes-cleaning products.",
