              "text": "Explain the role of functional groups and structural properties of soaps in their cleansing action."
            }
          ]
        },
        "topic_names": [
          "4.5 SOAPS AND DETERGENTS"
        ],
        "answer": "Soaps are sodium or potassium salts of long-chain carboxylic acids. Their cleansing action is entirely dependent on the structural properties of these molecules: having two ends with differing properties. The hydrophilic ionic end facilitates interaction with water, providing solubility. The hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail interacts specifically with non-polar oily substances. In water, these long-chain molecules orient themselves into clusters called micelles, where tails are protected in the interior and ionic ends are in the water. This structure emulsifies oily dirt, keeps the dirt suspended in the solution, and prevents re-deposition, enabling efficient cleaning.",
